d interesting  character for many of the  same reasons he was flawed produced some  terrific fights and was one of the best  character arcs in the series and still  is to this day iconic moments like a  sacrifice during the buu saga and his  acknowledgement for his loved ones and  his family  stand as some of the most memorable in  the series climaxes that were  intentionally built up too carefully  over  many years and then super did this  they repeated the exact same moment  again to  significantly less success repetition  can be a useful technique for the climax  but  not so much after this is sort of where  the phrase diminishing returns actually  comes from after the highs of the cell  saga gohan's character was never really  given a greater purpose  from the get-go he was polite and  friendly but his defining  characteristics that garnered him both  interest and fans were his  explosions of anger and after the cell  saga he couldn't depend  on that which resulted in a lot of  meandering for one reason or another  whether it be with an unearned power  boost to make him ultimate gohan  or even in super where his surprise  surprise got to relive key moments from  his younger years  but not before being thrown into the  spotlight only to be thrown  into the garbage in resurrection f  it was around this time online that a  lot of fans of gohan were really upset  and conversations surrounding the  character reached a fever pitch  and i think justifiably so with opposing  sides arguing voraciously however if  they have nothing for gohan as a  character  then sending him the way of yamcha  tenshinha and all the other side  characters  might be a good idea the spotlight  should be after all reserved for  meaningful progression of arcs and or  the plot  but super didn't do that and time  after time after time he is kept in this  strange nebulous position where he says  he's training  and improving but it almost always  happens off-screen  with any additional time we spend with  him feeling quite meaningless  and then suddenly the story starts to  care about gohan  as a character this is the section of  the story many fans will point to as the  moment gohan made a rebound as a  character and regained much of his  former glory in new  and interesting ways and to be honest i  think they're half right to be perfectly  clear  if you're someone who likes what they  did here during these latter sections of  the story i'm not about to say that  you're wrong for liking it i'm just here  to explain why i not only don't find it  great but find it to be one of the most  infuriating aspects of super's long list  of missteps and issues  but first i want to talk about something  i actually enjoyed with relatively no  reservations and that was piccolo and  gohan's fight during the tournament of  power against the universe 6 namekians  amidst the bombastic moments of the  tournament of power i sincerely felt as  though  this fight got overshadowed and  ultimately forgotten despite it being  one of the best character driven moments  in the entire series you see  one of the toughest things to deliver in  a story like dragon ball which isn't  planned out in advance is to build upon  previously successful character arcs in  both believable and meaningful ways  and that's a very difficult act to  balance if you force a character down an  interesting path  you run the risk of making it feel  artificial or forced and if you only  focus on how realistic the path  is you get a family man gohan which  while believable  wasn't very compelling to watch for me  in a show centered around conflict and i  don't envy the writers of the series at  all in this respect it's super tough  however in the anime vegeta's treatment  felt more like a novelty nostalgia act  during super  more than anything else but shockingly  those behind the story  eventually found something super  interesting  for vegeta to do interestingly as soon  as the manga surpassed the anime and  moved on to bigger and better things  vegeta became the recipient of some much  needed development that has made him  my favorite aspect of the super manga  today bouncing off of plot points rooted  deeply in his past  this new hurdle or issue vegeta needs to  overcome  has made him feel more like vegeta to me  than he has throughout all of super  wanting to atone for the sins of his  past and struggling with finding solace  in those transgressions is exactly the  sort of natural interesting progression  he  and this story needed after floundering  with the slideshow act for over 131  episodes and the crazy thing about this  is  gohan realistically wasn't nearly as  important or as prevalent of a character  during super and yet  they managed to offer gohan one of the  most interesting and natural  progressions  his character perhaps has ever received  this is one of the most interesting and  compelling plot points or arc ideas in  all of dragon ball i think dragon ball  doesn't make a habit of trying to be  profound or thought-provoking all  too often with characters like vegeta  piccolo and goku playing with concepts  like forgiveness at varying degrees and  exploring what that meant to each of  them in their positions within the world  however gohan is often in the  conversation too  boasting one of the most emphatic  coming-of-age stories in the series  however a fantastic video discussing and  exploring what gohan could possibly have  meant to us was brought to my attention  in early 2020 by  plague of gripes in it he explores the  introduction process  and fall of gohan's character through  the lens of fans that enjoyed him as an  empowerment fantasy and truth be told  this isn't a new concept it's actually  quite old but was used tremendous effect  during dragon ball to help connect young  fans to the character of gohan now i  think the gohan character has a  perfectly fine personality but  what attracted so many to him wasn't  necessarily his outward unassuming  composition but instead his ability to  flip the script on those that would try  to oppress him  and once the image of a young boy  struggling against monsters who would  underestimate and went away after cell  so too did his interest factor for  millions  so why didn't this happen with vegeta i  mean technically he lost his initial  interest factor too right  well personally i think vegeta succeeded  where gohan failed because his character  arc and development  specifically was rooted in his  relationships with other people  therefore when he tried to eventually  honestly atone for what he had done  it was cathartic yes but it also allowed  him the opportunity to explore the  character of family man vegeta which  while not super fun at least gave him  enough material to work with to warrant  being a major player  in the sequel series of super with goku  it was something new whereas gohan on  the other hand during the series  struggled to come to terms not with his  relationships but with himself  and it's great that he had this big  epiphany in the cell saga and it was  completely cathartic but  after that where does that leave him  when he inevitably has to relax as a  character  and bounce off of others the answer the  exact same place he had been before  because that epiphany he came to doesn't  really affect his relationships  all that much and certainly not the same  way vegeta's did and thus he disappeared  from the main story  until he came back the story that was  eventually offered up for gohan a  character that has almost entirely  vanished from the main plot threads  was another coming-of-age story however  this time it focused on  adulthood offering the audiences he grew  up alongside with another story or  struggle i'm sure many at some time or  another have grappled with  and that's arrogance with the best part  of this being  much like vegeta's new character act  rooted in his past gohan  now has a fitting believable and rooted  story where he must overcome the  personality flaw that has plagued him  and cost him on so many occasions in the  past a story where he must confront the  worst parts of himself to become the  stronger person  for the good of his family his friends  and himself  god i'm getting hyped just thinking  about it something like this has the  potential for being better and more  emotionally rich than the battle would  sell easily but how does super decide to  tell the story  well in short it doesn't gohan  versus goku episode 90 is one of the  best most beautiful  and most infuriatingly frustrating  episodes in  all of super's catalog for me all at the  exact same time  a phenomenal episode filled to bursting  with traditional dragon ball imagery  that looks as though it was designed  from the ground up to be the most iconic  climax to what was a long overdue story  to be told and it was frustrating  because it never received the build up  or substance a moment like this truly  deserved  which for me entirely sucked the life  out of it  for gohan in super i enjoyed his fight  with lavender i enjoyed his training  with piccolo and i enjoyed his fight  with goku however all of these take  place over the course of a few days in  the story  and a few episodes in reality making all  this time spent feel more like a  formality and leaving gohan feeling less  like a character  and more like a vessel for toei to fill  up with plot formula so that they can  use him for the tournament of power  later on  there's no time spent exploring his  mindset in any meaningful way  just a quick conversation with piccolo  who helps him find an impossibly quick  solution  and resolutions to a lifelong issue he  specifically has struggled with all  within  a single episode's b plot this could  have been one of the super anime's only  meaningful character arcs in the same  way andy and toy story 3 grew up  alongside the kids that watched toy  story 1  gohan's story could have provided a  mature and interesting exploration into  a problem  many of his older fans who grew up with  him share and they  in my opinion completely squandered it  with characters like vegeta who's had to  deal with arrogance in one way or  another  he could have offered tremendous  opportunities for conversation that  could have benefited them both  in the long term this could have given  gohan the impetus for a meaningful  change of character through natural and  believable progression  and it would have given vegeta a perfect  jumping off point for the arky would  eventually undergo  later on in super's manga with moro and  eventually granola  imagine a nuanced interesting concept  like struggling with arrogance in high  pressure situations with a mentor  character that struggled with it any  final fight against a father figure that  has found a way to float the balance  between arrogance and confidence  perfectly  to act as the final test before he must  leave his family to fight for the good  of everyone  in the most high pressure situation of  his young life  i mean it writes itself and it's  brilliant however instead of a long-term  story over the course of  months and years of struggle gohan  through a single confrontation with  piccolo  overcomes a fundamental and deeply  rooted personality flaw  all within the confines of a single  episode's b plot within dragon ball  super  making this feel less deserving and  moreover making gohan seem like an idiot  who didn't even bother trying to think  about it  at the end of the day after that dust  had settled and all the talk surrounding  my previous video came down what  surprised me the most was how readily  fans of the gohan character were willing  to accept any shine his character would  receive  as evidence of a resurgence when from my  point of view  this was only further evidence to the  contrary i adore gohan as a character  and like all of you watching his high  points in the series offer me some of  the most iconic and enjoyable from my  childhood  however as i hope i explained in this  video superficial window dressing  isn't enough to grant substance to a  character that has been bankrupt of  substance  in my opinion for an unjustified amount  of time all i have to do  is point to characters like piccolo  junior's introduction and his sacrifice  for gohan or  vegeta's ruthless lone wolf persona and  his eventual sacrifice in defense of  those he built relationships with to  show you all  what the benefit of time and thought  gives to a particular moment  it gives it substance it gives it weight  it makes  it meaningful and until such time  gohan's character receives that  he will unfortunately remain a hollow  character thanks for watching  run

